GRUVAX uses Alembic with the async psycopg driver.

Each requested migration chain is one PostgreSQL transaction. A downgrade that
crosses revision 0010 or drops revision 0009 first locks the GRUVAX profile tables
and checks for non-default profile data, before any migration SQL runs. The same
guard is the first operation in both downgrade functions for direct/emitted SQL.
It refuses rather than deleting or deduplicating user metadata, PATs, collections,
settings, boundaries, overrides or statistics. A profile with no boundaries is
still protected. Transaction-owned locks prevent a concurrent profile writer
from racing the check and are released by commit or rollback.

Back up/export non-default profiles before planning a destructive rollback; the
migration never removes them automatically. Default-only upgrade/downgrade
round-trips remain supported. A refusal or later failure leaves the entire
requested chain's revision, schema, constraints and data unchanged.
Revision 0018 adds profile-scoped, timestamped search/selection activity. Lifetime
counts and current default/global callers are preserved; no historical events
are fabricated from old aggregates. Exact seven-day tracking starts when 0018
is installed. Until seven days have elapsed, the window contains only activity
observed since that upgrade. The window is 168 elapsed hours and is recalculated
on reads, so dormant records decay without another write. Event history stores
only profile UUID, release ID, fixed event kind and time, never query text.

Lifetime increments and event insertion share one transaction. Expired derived
history is pruned on activity in that profile; active history and other profiles
are preserved. The existing global Reset stats action clears both history and
lifetime aggregates atomically. Compatibility _7d columns are refreshed on writes,
but diagnostic window counts come from actual timestamps rather than those caches.

A downgrade crossing 0018 refuses before any revision DDL if ANY event is still
inside its active window, including the default profile. Its direct/emitted SQL
also starts with that guard. Locks follow writer order (record_stats, then event
history) and remain held through the entire transaction. Expired-only history can
be discarded; 0018 -> 0017 -> head preserves lifetime totals and last-event times.
An active-history refusal or later-chain failure keeps revision/schema/data intact.
